# the tables of spir-v's core set of the target op catalog
#
# a core op is the instruction its row of masc's set is. the tables state what
# the ir knows a core op as, in the ir's own terms: the forms the target writes
# as it, over which values, or the part of a module's structure it is. they say
# nothing of a language: which ops a front end spells with a construct of its
# own, and the words for it, are the front end's (mach keeps them in
# src/lower/written.mach), since only it knows its constructs. an op the tables
# do not name is one the ir has no form for, which a target op alone writes.
# the tables also name the handle kinds masc's rows state for an id

use std.types.size.usize;

use spirv_kind: masc.isa.spirv.kind;

use mirl.ir.opcode.row.Opcode;
use opcodes: mirl.ir.opcode.table;
